description: 'v054''s recipe with the REGULARISATION/HEAD axis opened: inter-layer
  dropout 0.1, plus a BatchNorm+dropout head of width 256. The recurrent trunk (GRU
  2x128, input_proj 64) is untouched. Motivation is external: 97 submissions in the
  user''s OTHER competition (/Users/derricksamuel/Desktop/Projects/wunderfund) found
  batch_norm the single largest knob (mean val_r2 0.367 with vs 0.121 without, n=90/7),
  fc_num_layers=1 catastrophic (0.134, n=11) vs 2-4 layers (0.374-0.378), and dropout
  0.1-0.2 fine while 0.3 was bad (0.301). This project has used dropout=0.0 in all
  56 versions and never tried batch norm at all.'
hypothesis: Regularisation, not capacity, is the untested axis here. Three separate
  capacity increases have now failed to move public score (v045 2x wider student +0.0002,
  v026/v027 2x and 4x larger, negative), and v054 showed that removing a 4x train/serve
  memory mismatch changed nothing. If this also fails, the regularisation hypothesis
  is dead too and the 0.0111 gap is data or objective, not model. If it works it is
  the first gain in 56 versions from anything other than distillation.
change_vs_parent: 'vs v054: model.dropout 0.0 -> 0.1; model.head_hidden 192 -> 256;
  model.head_batch_norm False -> True; model.head_dropout 0.0 -> 0.1. GRU trunk, features,
  loss, optim, schedule, seed and state_reset all IDENTICAL to v054. Latency measured
  at 32.4 us/row ONNX-only under load vs 32.6 for the shipped 225,922-param shape,
  i.e. free.'
